00:09:19,926 --> 00:09:21,066
And those that do-

268
00:09:21,066 --> 00:09:22,112
- Will wish he were dead.

269
00:09:22,112 --> 00:09:24,296
- His sloppy speech.

270
00:09:24,296 --> 00:09:25,206
Do you know-

271
00:09:25,206 --> 00:09:27,090
- That I have little
bunnies painted on my knees?

272
00:09:27,090 --> 00:09:28,091
I do.

273
00:09:28,091 --> 00:09:29,274
- Most of the speakers we have heard

274
00:09:29,274 --> 00:09:30,932
can be traced to one thing.

275
00:09:30,932 --> 00:09:31,765
- Tight under things.

276
00:09:31,765 --> 00:09:34,348
- Practically all of us
would be better speakers

277
00:09:34,348 --> 00:09:37,684
if it were not for this one big fault.

278
00:09:37,684 --> 00:09:39,267
- Red car-lessness?

279
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:41,253
- Huh?

280
00:09:41,253 --> 00:09:42,137
- Yes, carelessness.

281
00:09:42,137 --> 00:09:43,870
- Oh, it just didn't look right.

282
00:09:43,870 --> 00:09:45,037
- Poor speech.

283
00:09:47,354 --> 00:09:49,056
If this speaker would concentrate

284
00:09:49,056 --> 00:09:53,090
upon overcoming his speech
fault of saying uh and ur,

285
00:09:53,090 --> 00:09:56,093
he could be an interesting
and effective speaker.

286
00:09:56,093 --> 00:09:59,327
Does carelessness make
you this type of speaker?

287
00:09:59,327 --> 00:10:00,590
- Think about it, won't you?

288
00:10:00,590 --> 00:10:01,694
Thank you.

289
00:10:01,694 --> 00:10:04,362
- [Professor] Now, this girl
is marked by carelessness-

290
00:10:04,362 --> 00:10:05,195
- And the devil.

291
00:10:05,195 --> 00:10:06,616
- [Professor] Because
she speeds over the words

292
00:10:06,616 --> 00:10:09,802
of her speech just to get them said,

293
00:10:09,802 --> 00:10:12,872
and she drops her voice
at the end of sentences.

294
00:10:12,872 --> 00:10:14,313
- And she drops to the floor

295
00:10:14,313 --> 00:10:17,822
and rolls her eyes into
the back of her head.

296
00:10:17,822 --> 00:10:19,275
- [Professor] Is carelessness making you

297
00:10:19,275 --> 00:10:20,977
this type of speaker?

298
00:10:20,977 --> 00:10:22,127
- Think about it, won't you?

299
00:10:22,127 --> 00:10:22,992
Thank you.

300
00:10:22,992 --> 00:10:25,485
- [Professor] Or is your
carelessness the same as that

301
00:10:25,485 --> 00:10:28,178
of the man who runs his words together?

302
00:10:28,178 --> 00:10:30,536
Mumbles, drops his Gs.

303
00:10:30,536 --> 00:10:34,971
- Drops his pants and
robs convenience stores.

304
00:10:34,971 --> 00:10:37,318
- Have you found yourself yet?

305
00:10:37,318 --> 00:10:40,402
Do you know what your
careless speech habits are?

306
00:10:40,402 --> 00:10:43,103
The important thing is
to recognize the trouble

307
00:10:43,103 --> 00:10:45,195
and then take steps to correct it.

308
00:10:45,195 --> 00:10:46,839
- And then crush it like a cockroach.

309
00:10:46,839 --> 00:10:49,035
- Now let's see if we can help you.

310
00:10:49,035 --> 00:10:51,739
- Don't open beer bottles with your teeth.

311
00:10:51,739 --> 00:10:53,006
- Should practice opening the mouth

312
00:10:53,006 --> 00:10:55,858
wider than usual as he or she speaks.

313
00:10:55,858 --> 00:10:56,935
- And fresh breath, please.

314
00:10:56,935 --> 00:10:57,811
- [Professor] In this way,

315
00:10:57,811 --> 00:10:59,467
the words can flow out,

316
00:10:59,467 --> 00:11:03,285
and the audience can easily hear you.

317
00:11:03,285 --> 00:11:06,379
Speak with life and animation,

318
00:11:06,379 --> 00:11:08,529
yet speak deliberately.

319
00:11:08,529 --> 00:11:10,768
Use plenty of lip and tongue actions.

320
00:11:10,768 --> 00:11:11,794
(laughing)

321
00:11:11,794 --> 00:11:13,393
- [Professor] So you
can annunciate clearly,

322
00:11:13,393 --> 00:11:15,560
so you will be understood.

323
00:11:16,567 --> 00:11:17,695
- And don't forget the wire rack.

324
00:11:17,695 --> 00:11:20,328
- [Professor] Speak in low full tones,

325
00:11:20,328 --> 00:11:22,308
so that what you say will be pleasing

326
00:11:22,308 --> 00:11:24,525
and appealing to the ear.

327
00:11:24,525 --> 00:11:25,358
- And potent.

328
00:11:25,358 --> 00:11:27,515
- [Professor] Don't let
your voice be high, shrill,

329
00:11:27,515 --> 00:11:31,830
and unpleasant just because
you're a little excited.

330
00:11:31,830 --> 00:11:34,268
Keep it low and pleasing.

331
00:11:34,268 --> 00:11:36,003
- I like it that way.

332
00:11:36,003 --> 00:11:37,659
- Well, here's young George Patton,

333
00:11:37,659 --> 00:11:39,696
a patriot and into high grade weed.

334
00:11:39,696 --> 00:11:41,352
- [Professor] So that each
sentence doesn't sound

335
00:11:41,352 --> 00:11:42,695
just like the other.

336
00:11:42,695 --> 00:11:43,731
- When David Duke is elected,

337
00:11:43,731 --> 00:11:46,169
we will take over the country.

338
00:11:46,169 --> 00:11:47,148
- [Professor] Manner of speaking must

339
00:11:47,148 --> 00:11:49,365
be pleasant and interesting.

340
00:11:49,365 --> 00:11:50,493
- Do I please you?

341
00:11:50,493 --> 00:11:51,643
- And finally, let your voice

342
00:11:51,643 --> 00:11:55,447
and speaking manner reflect
your own personality.

343
00:11:55,447 --> 00:11:57,838
Let it be sincere and typical of you,

344
00:11:57,838 --> 00:12:01,027
rather than stilted and stagey.

345
00:12:01,027 --> 00:12:01,860
- Like me.

346
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:02,729
(chuckling)

347
00:12:02,729 --> 00:12:06,737
- Ladies, I'm so glad to see you all here,

348
00:12:06,737 --> 00:12:08,722
and see you looking so well.

349
00:12:08,722 --> 00:12:09,872
- It's an Aunt Bea convention.

350
00:12:09,872 --> 00:12:13,662
- Our subject this afternoon is flowers.

351
00:12:13,662 --> 00:12:16,079
Don't you just adore flowers?

352
00:12:16,981 --> 00:12:18,414
- She is one hot mama.

353
00:12:18,414 --> 00:12:20,254
- Do you see what I mean?

354
00:12:20,254 --> 00:12:23,060
Now what she really meant to say was this.

355
00:12:23,060 --> 00:12:24,465
- My life is a hollow lie.

356
00:12:24,465 --> 00:12:26,384
- That you've come out today, ladies.

357
00:12:26,384 --> 00:12:28,660
Glad to see you looking so well.

358
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:31,018
The subject of our discussion is flowers,

359
00:12:31,018 --> 00:12:33,836
one of which I'm particularly fond.

360
00:12:33,836 --> 00:12:35,504
- Poppies.
- I know that many of you too

361
00:12:35,504 --> 00:12:38,318
share my great enthusiasm
for this subject.

362
00:12:38,318 --> 00:12:41,518
- The idea is to be interesting.

363
00:12:41,518 --> 00:12:43,300
Interesting, but real.

364
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:45,335
- Firm yet yielding to the touch.

365
00:12:45,335 --> 00:12:47,210
- Now are there any questions?
- Professor Buehler?

366
00:12:47,210 --> 00:12:48,569
- [Professor] Yes?

367
00:12:48,569 --> 00:12:50,225
- Won't people think I'm peculiar

368
00:12:50,225 --> 00:12:53,491
if I start speaking differently
than I ever have before?

369
00:12:53,491 --> 00:12:54,324
- People think that already.

370
00:12:54,324 --> 00:12:56,343
- Now that's a good point,

371
00:12:56,343 --> 00:12:58,560
but I don't think you need worry about it.

372
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:01,817
You see, your speaking voice
will become more adequate.

373
00:13:01,817 --> 00:13:03,427
- Sounds like Brenda Vacarro.

374
00:13:03,427 --> 00:13:04,807
- Just the rules of good speaking.

375
00:13:04,807 --> 00:13:07,416
And your friends won't
think that you're peculiar.

376
00:13:07,416 --> 00:13:08,495
On the other hand,

377
00:13:08,495 --> 00:13:10,290
they're more likely to admire you.

378
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:12,108
- For having the courage
to come out in public.

379
00:13:12,108 --> 00:13:14,580
- having improved your manner of speaking.

380
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:17,398
Speak audibly so you may be heard.

381
00:13:17,398 --> 00:13:18,845
- John Heard.

382
00:13:18,845 --> 00:13:22,090
- Speak distinctly so
you may be understood.

383
00:13:22,090 --> 00:13:23,342
- [All] Huh?

384
00:13:23,342 --> 00:13:25,240
- And speak with life and enthusiasm,

385
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:27,564
so you may be pleasing.

386
00:13:27,564 --> 00:13:29,831
- And get yourself a good wire rack.

387
00:13:29,831 --> 00:13:32,336
- And remember that good speech will be

388
00:13:32,336 --> 00:13:34,532
a great asset to you always.

389
00:13:34,532 --> 00:13:37,338
It is the mark of a cultured person

390
00:13:37,338 --> 00:13:39,445
and a well-developed personality.

391
00:13:39,445 --> 00:13:40,604
- And a good friend.

392
00:13:40,604 --> 00:13:45,480
- Take pride in your voice
and your speaking manner.

393
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:47,549
Take pride in the way you sound.

394
00:13:47,549 --> 00:13:48,712
- Put out some chips or something.

395
00:13:48,712 --> 00:13:50,172
Didn't your mother teach you anything?

396
00:13:50,172 --> 00:13:53,229
- Be careful how you use it.

397
00:13:53,229 --> 00:13:55,302
(patriotic music)

398
00:13:55,302 --> 00:13:57,898
- Look, there's a hammer and
a sickle in that eye, beware!

399
00:13:57,898 --> 00:14:00,442
- Yay, speech, speech!

400
00:14:00,442 --> 00:14:02,629
Speech, woo, speech!
(whistling)

401
00:14:02,629 --> 00:14:03,490
- Speech?

402
00:14:03,490 --> 00:14:05,605
- Speech, lip and tongue action, woo!

403
00:14:05,605 --> 00:14:06,605
Woo, author!

404
00:14:08,448 --> 00:14:09,281
(laughing)

405
00:14:09,281 --> 00:14:11,864
(upbeat music)

406
00:14:16,428 --> 00:14:17,928
- Say, impressive.

407
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,478
This film's a capitol idea.

408
00:14:22,478 --> 00:14:24,228
The seal of approval.

409
00:14:25,100 --> 00:14:26,351
Well, good night, everybody, got to go,

410
00:14:26,351 --> 00:14:27,184
- Oh.

411
00:14:28,667 --> 00:14:29,985
- Made you look.

412
00:14:29,985 --> 00:14:33,152
- Hey Charlotte, it spells out a word.

413
00:14:36,173 --> 00:14:37,874
- Earth Versus by Walt Whitman.

414
00:14:37,874 --> 00:14:39,277
- No, no, no, no.

415
00:14:39,277 --> 00:14:42,360
- I'm putting my money on the spider.

416
00:14:47,465 --> 00:14:50,251
- Hey, this film's
slipping us a Mickey, what?

417
00:14:50,251 --> 00:14:52,501
- Rather be slipped a Finn.

418
00:14:53,482 --> 00:14:54,315
- No.

419
00:14:56,399 --> 00:14:58,332
- Hey, do you bet that
Jack Webb's in this?

420
00:14:58,332 --> 00:14:59,832
- Oh, that's cute.

421
00:15:01,690 --> 00:15:04,713
- This spider keeps seeing the credits.

422
00:15:04,713 --> 00:15:07,879
- Shirley Falls and it's not my fault.

423
00:15:08,714 --> 00:15:10,381
It's Earl Pelbert's.

424
00:15:12,649 --> 00:15:15,028
- Hey, a rolling stone
gathers no Marty Moss.

425
00:15:15,028 --> 00:15:16,361
- I suppose not.

426
00:15:17,399 --> 00:15:18,457
- It's just a credit line.

427
00:15:18,457 --> 00:15:20,674
- Hey, Paul Michael's father.

428
00:15:20,674 --> 00:15:22,284
- That's Glaser, Glaser.

429
00:15:22,284 --> 00:15:23,117
- Oh.

430
00:15:23,953 --> 00:15:25,587
- Oh, assistant technical effects?

431
00:15:25,587 --> 00:15:27,645
Oh, honey, hand me that Tonka truck.

432
00:15:27,645 --> 00:15:28,954
- What a burn.

433
00:15:28,954 --> 00:15:30,185
- Gorog write screenplay.

434
00:15:30,185 --> 00:15:33,935
(primeval grunting)

435
00:15:33,935 --> 00:15:36,373
- No spiders were squished,
stepped on, flushed,

436
00:15:36,373 --> 00:15:38,363
or made to suffer any emotional distress

437
00:15:38,363 --> 00:15:39,789
during the making of this film.

438
00:15:39,789 --> 00:15:41,398
One spider did die of old age.

439
00:15:41,398 --> 00:15:45,814
We have two letters from
doctors confirming this.

440
00:15:45,814 --> 00:15:46,897
- Scared yet?

441
00:15:48,366 --> 00:15:49,199
- I'm shaking.

442
00:15:49,199 --> 00:15:50,866
I'm shaking, people.

443
00:15:51,921 --> 00:15:53,255
They're not very proud of these people.

444
00:15:53,255 --> 00:15:54,755
- No one would be.

445
00:15:57,060 --> 00:16:00,017
- Our spider seems to
be shorting out, sir.

446
00:16:00,017 --> 00:16:01,351
- It's blocking the union seal there.

447
00:16:01,351 --> 00:16:02,350
- It's getting too close.

448
00:16:02,350 --> 00:16:06,803
- Come into my parlor said
the spider to the titles.

449
00:16:06,803 --> 00:16:09,136
- Spider by Spencer's Gifts.

450
00:16:12,019 --> 00:16:13,436
- Anytown, U.S.A.

451
00:16:15,172 --> 00:16:16,005
- Good place.

452
00:16:16,005 --> 00:16:17,426
- Hey, you're driving on the sidewalk.

453
00:16:17,426 --> 00:16:18,593
Hey, hey, hey!

454
00:16:20,416 --> 00:16:21,999
- I'm liquid metal.

455
00:16:22,891 --> 00:16:24,476
- Let me tell you a
little bit about myself.

456
00:16:24,476 --> 00:16:25,309
I drive a truck,

457
00:16:25,309 --> 00:16:26,142
I'm butt ugly,

458
00:16:26,142 --> 00:16:27,725
and I hate spiders.

459
00:16:32,413 --> 00:16:34,253
My lips are big.

460
00:16:34,253 --> 00:16:35,366
- That dad of mine.

461
00:16:35,366 --> 00:16:36,265
He's so thoughtful,

462
00:16:36,265 --> 00:16:37,932
and a nightgown too.

463
00:16:41,987 --> 00:16:44,372
(screaming)

464
00:16:44,372 --> 00:16:46,222
- Hey, he filled the light with.

465
00:16:46,222 --> 00:16:50,077
- I'm guessing the film is not about him.

466
00:16:50,077 --> 00:16:51,169
- We're sorry that had to happen,

467
00:16:51,169 --> 00:16:55,505
now let's get back to our
story in Cleantown, USA.

468
00:16:55,505 --> 00:16:57,216
- Why don't I feel fresh?
- Carol?

469
00:16:57,216 --> 00:16:58,528
- Hey Carol, wait.

470
00:16:58,528 --> 00:17:00,322
- Hi, Goonie.
- Hi, Michael.

471
00:17:00,322 --> 00:17:01,439
- Did you hear about your dad?

472
00:17:01,439 --> 00:17:02,272
- Hey, wake up.

473
00:17:02,272 --> 00:17:03,831
This is Mike, remember?

474
00:17:03,831 --> 00:17:05,481
The boy with the golden heart
and the memory of an elephant.

475
00:17:05,481 --> 00:17:07,971
- And the teeth of a chipmunk.

476
00:17:07,971 --> 00:17:09,397
- Look, I remembered, see?

477
00:17:09,397 --> 00:17:10,981
- Something to remember your dad by.

478
00:17:10,981 --> 00:17:12,375
- Happy birthday, Carol.

479
00:17:12,375 --> 00:17:13,208
- Thanks.

480
00:17:17,134 --> 00:17:18,226
- Aren't you going to open it?

481
00:17:18,226 --> 00:17:21,089
- I'll have to return it first.

482
00:17:21,089 --> 00:17:22,826
- What's wrong?

483
00:17:22,826 --> 00:17:24,460
- It's Dad.

484
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:25,632
He didn't come home last night.

485
00:17:25,632 --> 00:17:26,465
- So what?

486
00:17:26,465 --> 00:17:27,656
You know it doesn't mean a thing.

487
00:17:27,656 --> 00:17:29,863
- If it ain't got that swing.
- You know how he is.

488
00:17:29,863 --> 00:17:31,493
- Yeah, he's a boozy drunkard.

489
00:17:31,493 --> 00:17:32,326
- Oh!

490
00:17:32,326 --> 00:17:33,936
- What do you mean I know how he is?

491
00:17:33,936 --> 00:17:35,350
- Slap him.

492
00:17:35,350 --> 00:17:36,183
- Nothing.

493
00:17:40,799 --> 00:17:42,041
(horn honking)

494
00:17:42,041 --> 00:17:43,400
- Ah, just annoying my girlfriend.

495
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:45,150
I'm sorry, excuse me.

496
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:50,753
- Oh, you're right.

497
00:17:50,753 --> 00:17:52,586
My dad's gutter swill.

498
00:17:54,190 --> 00:17:56,726
- Well he went to Springdale
yesterday to buy me a present.

499
00:17:56,726 --> 00:17:58,912
He said he'd be back
before dinner last night,

500
00:17:58,912 --> 00:18:00,742
but he still hadn't come back yet.

501
00:18:00,742 --> 00:18:02,030
- That doesn't mean a thing, Carol.

502
00:18:02,030 --> 00:18:03,698
I mean, it isn't the first time.

503
00:18:03,698 --> 00:18:05,270
- Doh!

504
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:06,523
- You're mean, Mike.

505
00:18:06,523 --> 00:18:07,642
- That's why I love you.

506
00:18:07,642 --> 00:18:09,031
- I don't want your silly present either.

507
00:18:09,031 --> 00:18:09,864
Here!

508
00:18:11,079 --> 00:18:13,662
♪ Dobey, Dobey ♪

509
00:18:16,382 --> 00:18:17,465
- That Dobey.

510
00:18:18,602 --> 00:18:21,442
- No, teacher, no, I'm
sorry I talked in class!

511
00:18:21,442 --> 00:18:23,570
(imitating electricity zapping)

512
00:18:23,570 --> 00:18:24,604
- Okay, who's next?

513
00:18:24,604 --> 00:18:26,477
- Electrons jump from one
electrode to the other

514
00:18:26,477 --> 00:18:28,741
because of the difference
in the electrical potential

515
00:18:28,741 --> 00:18:30,792
between the two poles.
(snoring)

516
00:18:30,792 --> 00:18:32,516
Now this one is the negative pole,

517
00:18:32,516 --> 00:18:35,692
on which electricity flows
to the positive pole.

518
00:18:35,692 --> 00:18:37,314
We call the positive pole what?

519
00:18:37,314 --> 00:18:38,728
- Les qualensza.

520
00:18:38,728 --> 00:18:39,561
- Correct.

521
00:18:39,561 --> 00:18:40,394
The negative pole?

522
00:18:40,394 --> 00:18:41,227
- [All] The axle.

523
00:18:41,227 --> 00:18:42,880
- Now what don't I know my butt from?

524
00:18:42,880 --> 00:18:43,776
- [Both] A hole in the ground.

525
00:18:43,776 --> 00:18:44,628
- [Kingman] These electrodes is increased

526
00:18:44,628 --> 00:18:46,558
to a greater amplitude,

527
00:18:46,558 --> 00:18:49,097
- I'm submissive in a '50s kind of way.

528
00:18:49,097 --> 00:18:50,169
- [Kingman] The larger the arc becomes,

529
00:18:50,169 --> 00:18:51,169
the more dangerous it is

530
00:18:51,169 --> 00:18:54,389
to anything that may
be placed in its path.

531
00:18:54,389 --> 00:18:56,459
One thing to remember is
that the flow of electrons

532
00:18:56,459 --> 00:18:58,980
is always from the
negative to the positive.

533
00:18:58,980 --> 00:19:00,599
The reason for this is
